<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penn Museum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as University of Pennsylvania Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Penn Museum, formally the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology, is a museum and research institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> founded in 1887. It has sponsored excavations across the world, including at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, at Hasanlu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and at sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. Its collections number around a million objects.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penn-museum.jpg" alt="Penn Museum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ajay_suresh · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Penn_Museum_-_UPenn_(53590175396).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The museum was founded after a successful expedition to the ancient site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, when Provost William Pepper persuaded the trustees of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> to erect a fireproof building for its finds. Like other museums of the time, it shared the ownership of excavated objects with the host countries, so most of its holdings have a known archaeological context. Its building, in the Arts and Crafts and Eclectic style, was designed by Wilson Eyre, Cope &amp; Stewardson and Frank Miles Day, and is only about a third of the original plan. The first phase was completed in 1899, the rotunda in 1915 and the Sharpe Wing in 1929.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The collections fall into three divisions: archaeology, ethnology and physical anthropology. Among the most important are the finds from the Royal Tombs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, co-excavated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, including crowns, figures and the Bull-headed lyre, and almost 30,000 clay tablets in Sumerian and Akkadian cuneiform, among them the largest number of Sumerian school tablets of any museum. A museum expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1915 brought a 13-ton granite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sphinx">Sphinx</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> from the palace of Merenptah, and the museum excavated the Maya city of Tikal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> from 1956 to 1970. Its scientists have conducted more than 300 expeditions, and it has published Expedition magazine since 1958.</p><p>The Museum Library began in 1900 with the personal library of Daniel Garrison Brinton, professor of American archaeology and linguistics. Under Cynthia Griffin, its first full-time librarian from 1942, it was opened to students and grew from nearly 20,000 volumes in 1945 to over 46,000 in 1965.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In November 2008 the administration cut 18 research specialist posts and closed the Museum Applied Science Center for Archaeology, a step scholars criticised as a departure from its mission and which administrators attributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2008-financial-crisis">2008 financial crisis</a>.</p><p>The museum holds about 196 objects taken in the Benin Expedition of 1897, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a>, and has said it is open to returning them. It also holds about 1,300 skulls gathered by the physician Samuel George Morton, long criticised for using science to uphold racism; in 2018 students found that 55 were of enslaved people, and in July 2020 the museum moved the collection into storage. In April 2021 the museum and the university apologised for allowing remains of victims of the 1985 MOVE bombing to be used in research and teaching.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Penn Museum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…region, was first dug for six weeks by Robert Koldewey in 1887, who took it for an extended burial place; Thorkild Jacobsen and Fuad Safar found the first evidence of its identification as Lagash in 1953, and excavation resumed in 2019 under Holly Pittman of the <b>Penn Museum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The treasures are now divided between the British Museum, the <b>Penn Museum</b> and the Baghdad Museum, and the partly restored ziggurat, built by Ur-Nammu and repaired by Ishme-Dagan of Isin, Kurigalzu I and Nabonidus, remains the best-preserved landmark of the site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Penn Museum</b> excavations under Robert Dyson (1956 to 1977) found hundreds of bodies, including the pair known as the 'Hasanlu Lovers'.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasanlu">Hasanlu</a></li></ul></section>
